The issue is with checkIptables assuming that iptables is installed. When iptables is not installed the function will always throw an exception and return ipTablesIsRunning=true.
/ambari-agent/src/main/python/ambari_agent/HostInfo.py
{code} def checkIptables(self):
    iptablesIsRunning = True
    try:
      iptables = subprocess.Popen([&quot;iptables&quot;, &quot;-S&quot;], stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
      stdout = iptables.communicate()
      if stdout == (&apos;-P INPUT ACCEPT\n-P FORWARD ACCEPT\n-P OUTPUT ACCEPT\n&apos;, None):
        iptablesIsRunning = False
    except:
      pass
    return iptablesIsRunning{code}

Looks like this is due to a recent change in rrd.py.  The call ...

{code}
urlparse.parse_qs(postData)
